  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nAn upper level disturbance tracking across the region through the \nday today will result in quite a cool day behind a cold front moving \nthrough as of publishing this discussion. Upper level support will \nyield a few showers through the day today, mainly north of the I-90 \ncorridor. Overall precipitation amounts with any shower that forms \nlooks to be not much more than a few hundreths. \n\nBy tomorrow another upper level ridge begins to work in from the \nwest. That said, a cyclonic northwesterly flow aloft over the \nNorthern Rockies will act to temper the warming trend, at least for \na day or two before the ridge fully moves overhead toward the latter \nhalf of the week. After today the region looks dry as the upper \nlevel ridge slowly builds in. \n\nFriday looks to be the hottest day at this time, with breezy winds \nover much of the region by mid-afternoon. Should this scenario \nunfold, concerns for fire weather conditions would be increase. \n\nConfidence in specifics wanes into next weekend, though there does \nlook to be some form of zonal flow aloft in place. The uncertainty \nlies in whether there will be any embedded waves capable of \nproducing additional stretches of gusty winds or precipitation \nshould they be strong enough. -AM\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nLargest source of uncertainty for this forecast will be just how \nbreezy and dry it will be on Friday across many areas. Ensemble \naverage 700 mb flow is on the order of 15 kts or so. Given the \nexpected deep mixing on Friday, these winds should not struggle to \nmix to the surface. Should this mid level flow trend stronger, \ncloser to the current 75th percentile (20-25 kts), there would be \nincreased concern for at least elevated fire weather Friday. -AM",
